99
00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:11.760
Like any human being who was alive at the time, I was an enormous fan of The Office.

100
00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:13.540
I just thought it was the greatest thing on television.

101
00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:20.760
It was this collection of the greatest talent, actors and writers who were making a generation

102
00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:25.660
defining comedy, which was somehow sweet, but always subversive.

103
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.260
And the storytelling was great.

104
00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:30.900
You know, feature directors that I really admired had already come and directed the

105
00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:31.920
show, like Harold Ramis.

106
00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.560
I think I had already directed Rain in Juno.

107
00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:39.000
And I had these little connections to other people on the show.

108
00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.900
I feel like I knew a fair amount of the cast and some writers on the show already.

109
00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.260
I don't even know why.

110
00:04:45.880 --> 00:04:51.300
But either my agent reached out and said, hey, do you want to direct any television?

111
00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:53.880
And I think my response was, I would be up for directing The Office.

112
00:04:53.880 --> 00:05:01.000
And Rain made it even sound like a better opportunity by saying, look, the worst you can do when you

113
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:05.140
direct The Office is get a B+, and the best you can do is an A.

114
00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:06.940
Well, all right.

115
00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.200
I mean, I always used to tell people that directing The Office was like someone asking you, do you

116
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.420
