138
00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.500
every camera angle that can be used in the editing of a scene.

139
00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:40.920
So this was a unique challenge as a director and kind of a thrilling one where you knew

140
00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.820
that there was eight shots you wanted to get off, a wide, a couple of mediums, close-ups,

141
00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.660
and you wanted to be able to pan into reactions.

142
00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:52.440
And that somehow you had two cameras, you had two operators, and they were going to need to

143
00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.940
catch all these things without catching each other.

144
00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:57.640
And they would duck under desks as one would pan by the other.

145
00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:02.060
It was like doing Sudoku, you know, it was like this fun problem to solve.

146
00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:06.660
And at the same time, a thrill to watch the actual operators who knew how to do it.

147
00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:11.340
I remember the set was freezing cold, which was Steve Carell's request, if I remember

148
00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.320
