498
00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:35.320
Mm-hmm.

499
00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:36.180
I see it.

500
00:24:36.280 --> 00:24:38.720
They both go upright with their arms out.

501
00:24:39.000 --> 00:24:40.240
It's very creepy.

502
00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:41.420
I know.

503
00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:45.440
Anyone else that watch Raised by Wolves, go to 3 minutes 39 seconds.

504
00:24:45.620 --> 00:24:49.140
Watch how Dwight does his liftoff, and it will remind you of Mother.

505
00:24:49.480 --> 00:24:51.220
Angela will also put that on the pod.

506
00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:52.920
I'm going to put it in our stories.

507
00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:55.640
I looked up Dwight's profile on Second Life.

508
00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:56.320
You did?

509
00:24:56.320 --> 00:24:57.100
I did.

510
00:24:57.240 --> 00:24:57.920
It's still there.

511
00:24:58.060 --> 00:25:00.200
Here's what he has down as his interests.

512
00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:04.620
Muscle cars, pop culture, and sci-fi buff.

513
00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:15.180
In parentheses, Harry Potter, The Lord of the Rings, Star Wars 24, Alias, Smallville, The Apprentice, Lost, and Battlestar Galactica.

514
00:25:15.440 --> 00:25:23.220
His talents are listed as avid corn husk doll maker, beat sculptor, and knot-tying champion.

515
00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:32.400
He had to list a goal for his second life, and he wrote to calculate the exchange between shrewd bucks and linden dollars.

516
00:25:32.940 --> 00:25:35.740
Linden dollars are the currency in second life.

517
00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:36.380
Okay.

518
00:25:36.380 --> 00:25:37.800
So there you have it.

519
00:25:37.900 --> 00:25:45.320
When he's not flying like Mother, he is trying to calculate the exchange between shrewd bucks and linden dollars.

520
00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:46.440
I love that.

521
00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:51.320
Up next, Michael is going to pitch his big idea to the ad guys.

522
00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:58.000
I want you guys to know a deleted scene would have come before this between Jim and Michael that is so delicious and fun.

523
00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:01.140
Basically, Michael wants to practice pitch to Jim.

524
00:26:01.380 --> 00:26:03.340
He brings Jim in the office, right?

525
00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:07.360
He has Jim sit behind his desk so he can practice pitch.

526
00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:10.100
And he says, this is his pitch to Jim.

527
00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:11.940
We start on a blank piece of paper.

528
00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:15.040
We widen to reveal ancient Rome.

529
00:26:15.540 --> 00:26:16.960
Jim's like, can't do that.

530
00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:18.260
Michael's like, okay.

531
00:26:18.520 --> 00:26:23.000
We widen to reveal a spaceship blasting out of a woman's womb.

532
00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:23.720
What?

533
00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:27.820
Then they do this very funny role play where Jim's like, get out of my office.

534
00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:29.320
It's like becomes a whole thing.

535
00:26:29.540 --> 00:26:33.680
But for real, the pitch Michael settles on is this one.

536
00:26:33.760 --> 00:26:35.260
And I thought we had to hear it.

537
00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:36.680
Sam, can you play it?

538
00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:40.640
Little girl in a field, holding a flower.

539
00:26:40.920 --> 00:26:44.280
We zoom back to find that she's in the desert.

540
00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:46.720
And the field is an oasis.

541
00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:48.300
We zoom back further.

542
00:26:48.980 --> 00:26:53.860
The desert is a sandbox in the world's largest resort hotel.

543
00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:55.240
Zoom back further.

544
00:26:55.540 --> 00:27:01.600
The hotel is actually the playground of the world's largest prison.

545
00:27:02.200 --> 00:27:03.620
But we zoom back further.

546
00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:05.740
I can tell that your time is valuable.

547
00:27:05.740 --> 00:27:10.660
Oh, well, I love what he says when the guy is like, listen, I can tell your time is valuable.

548
00:27:10.660 --> 00:27:14.060
And Michael's like, well, actually, I don't get paid by the hour anymore.

549
00:27:14.100 --> 00:27:15.380
I get paid by the year.

550
00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:18.460
So basically, I can waste as much time as I want.

551
00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:19.620
Not a problem.

552
00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:20.500
Yes.

553
00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:23.380
But I love that this is the pitch that he led with.

554
00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:27.380
And knowing that he had other ideas, but this is what he went with.

555
00:27:27.800 --> 00:27:29.120
The ad guys are great.

556
00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:31.720
They're so real in how they react to Michael.

557
00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:35.380
They say, listen, we made something for the Nashua branch.

558
00:27:35.620 --> 00:27:36.900
Why don't we show that to you?

559
00:27:37.140 --> 00:27:39.320
And it'll give you an idea of what we can do.

560
00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:41.640
So he plays this commercial.

561
00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:47.980
And then they explain the only part of the commercial they're allowed to alter is the last five seconds.

562
00:27:48.280 --> 00:27:48.440
Yeah.

563
00:27:48.540 --> 00:27:49.180
And Michael's like, what?

564
00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:50.380
We just wave at the end?

565
00:27:50.540 --> 00:27:52.180
He goes, you know, you could clap.

566
00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:53.300
You know, make it your own.

567
00:27:53.440 --> 00:27:53.580
Yeah.

568
00:27:53.660 --> 00:27:55.480
This is where you can really get creative.

569
00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:57.060
You can be inside.

570
00:27:57.080 --> 00:27:58.120
You can be outside.

571
00:27:58.120 --> 00:27:59.920
We had a fan question.

572
00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:03.920
Aaron Jassa, Harry Driscoll, Molly Coates, and many others.

573
00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:08.580
They wanted to know who directed this commercial that plays in the middle of the episode.

574
00:28:08.740 --> 00:28:09.980
Was it Jason Reitman?

575
00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:10.860
Yes.

576
00:28:11.060 --> 00:28:12.280
It was Jason Reitman.

577
00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:14.840
I texted with BJ about this commercial.

578
00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:20.400
And he told me that Jason Reitman had directed real commercials before this.

579
00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:29.740
And he remembers that it was so cool because Jason laid out this big circular track on the floor and had the camera going around in circles.

580
00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:33.240
And BJ was like, I had never seen anything like that before.

581
00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:34.400
It was so cool.

582
00:28:34.400 --> 00:28:39.480
Well, we asked Jason about filming this fake commercial, and here's what he had to say.

583
00:28:40.260 --> 00:28:43.640
The episode local ad contained two commercials inside it.

584
00:28:43.700 --> 00:28:44.640
And I had been a commercial director.

585
00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:46.560
I was a bad commercial director, I should say.

586
00:28:46.780 --> 00:28:52.420
You know, the majority of the commercials that I directed were ones that if you saw, you would revoke my director's guild card.

587
00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:54.000
You would just say, you're not allowed to direct again.

588
00:28:54.180 --> 00:28:57.640
And then I would, you know, name clients, but that would be rude.

589
00:28:57.640 --> 00:29:04.020
But, you know, typical fast food and beer and just, but like, not good work.

590
00:29:04.180 --> 00:29:05.620
I was not Spike Jonze.

591
00:29:05.680 --> 00:29:07.080
But I learned that format.

592
00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:10.800
There's certain things that you may not know how to do authentically, but you know how to satirize.

593
00:29:11.140 --> 00:29:20.180
Like, I know that I could never direct a David Fincher film, but I love him so much as a filmmaker that I could probably direct a satire of a David Fincher film.

594
00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:25.000
And so I don't know how to direct a great commercial, but I think I know how to direct the satire of a good commercial.

595
00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:31.720
And that was the kind of the great thing about doing the Dunder Mifflin ad was I felt my commercial brain trying to do the best,

596
00:29:31.840 --> 00:29:36.960
worst version of what Dunder Mifflin would hand Michael Scott, which was this kind of cutesy commercial.

597
00:29:37.180 --> 00:29:40.700
It starred Jeff Witzke, who is an actor I've worked with since I was a kid.

598
00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:43.820
He literally was in my first short film and has been in everything I've ever made since.

599
00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:44.980
And he was the star of it.

600
00:29:45.060 --> 00:29:45.460
And that was great.

601
00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:50.380
I love that Jason just uses his friend Jeff in everything.

602
00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:51.760
I just think that's wonderful.

603
00:29:52.060 --> 00:29:54.520
Jenna, I want you to be in everything I do from here on out.

604
00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:55.600
Great.

605
00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:57.140
I love it.

606
00:29:57.480 --> 00:29:57.720
Okay.

607
00:29:58.340 --> 00:30:01.100
Well, we have some crossover connections with Jeff.

608
00:30:01.320 --> 00:30:01.940
Are you ready?

609
00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:08.740
He was also in Mindy and Brenda, the TV short written by Mindy Kaling and Brenda Withers.

610
00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:09.600
Oh.

611
00:30:09.840 --> 00:30:10.140
Yeah.

612
00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:11.820
He played the sales clerk.

613
00:30:12.100 --> 00:30:13.960
Well, lady, guess what?

614
00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:17.080
He also did a guest spot on Monk.

615
00:30:17.220 --> 00:30:21.100
He played Human Cannonball in Mr. Monk Goes to the Circus.

616
00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:22.520
That one really got me.

617
00:30:22.520 --> 00:30:24.300
I might need to look that one up.

618
00:30:24.460 --> 00:30:25.360
Human Cannonball.

619
00:30:25.560 --> 00:30:26.200
Very curious.

620
00:30:26.500 --> 00:30:28.820
You might need to see what the Human Cannonball looks like.

621
00:30:29.020 --> 00:30:29.420
Yes.

622
00:30:29.720 --> 00:30:34.860
So not only did Jason hire Jeff again, he also hired a friend that he had worked with

623
00:30:34.860 --> 00:30:37.000
before to compose that music.

624
00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:40.820
That sort of like, I don't know, it's like salsa music that's playing in the background.

625
00:30:40.840 --> 00:30:47.400
It was composed by the same composer that did all the music for Juno, Matteo Messina.

626
00:30:47.400 --> 00:30:48.900
I love that.

627
00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:52.020
Now, things are going to get awkward.

628
00:30:52.760 --> 00:30:58.100
Andy pulls Dwight aside to get advice on how to get to first base with Angela.

629
00:30:58.400 --> 00:31:03.520
He says they've been necking a lot, but that means that they are literally rubbing their

630
00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:04.240
necks together.

631
00:31:04.460 --> 00:31:05.940
There's no kissing involved.

632
00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:08.060
Why don't we have any footage of that?

633
00:31:08.840 --> 00:31:10.220
I don't know.

634
00:31:10.680 --> 00:31:11.860
I don't know.

635
00:31:12.100 --> 00:31:15.020
Angela, we had a fan question from Amy Noki.

636
00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:21.440
She's dying to know, what is your backstory for Angela and Andy, and what is with the

637
00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:21.900
necking?

638
00:31:22.040 --> 00:31:26.920
Do you think this was standard for Angela, or is it because she can't stomach Andy and

639
00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:28.960
she's just trying to get back at Dwight?

640
00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:30.360
I love this question.

641
00:31:30.580 --> 00:31:34.900
I thought that Angela went out with Andy as sort of a revenge date.

642
00:31:35.060 --> 00:31:36.780
She was very mad at Dwight.

643
00:31:37.040 --> 00:31:38.940
She really thought she was done with Dwight.

644
00:31:39.060 --> 00:31:40.760
She was super, super angry with him.

645
00:31:40.760 --> 00:31:45.780
And as she started dating Andy, I think it became very clear to her that really Dwight

646
00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:49.040
had her heart, but she was too prideful to admit that.

647
00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:53.080
So she tried to date Andy, but it was a chore.

648
00:31:53.340 --> 00:31:57.660
And I think the necking thing is just something she was doing to put him off.

649
00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:01.440
Like, how can I let him touch me, but not really touch me?

650
00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:05.140
So yeah, I think her heart wasn't in it from the beginning.

651
00:32:05.140 --> 00:32:07.420
Yeah, I was thinking about it.

652
00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:15.220
And you know, Angela can be very fussy and very structured, but we saw her going at it

653
00:32:15.220 --> 00:32:19.880
in Jim's little toy playhouse thing and email surveillance.

654
00:32:19.880 --> 00:32:25.460
So when she's into someone, it's not like she puts people through these weird paces of

655
00:32:25.460 --> 00:32:27.700
like, okay, first we just rub our necks together.

656
00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:30.020
I think this was exclusive to Andy.

657
00:32:30.300 --> 00:32:30.760
Oh, yeah.

658
00:32:31.060 --> 00:32:31.220
Yeah.

659
00:32:31.420 --> 00:32:32.860
And maybe the senator too.

660
00:32:33.160 --> 00:32:33.500
Perhaps.

661
00:32:33.500 --> 00:32:35.680
I really think Dwight was the love of her life.

662
00:32:35.820 --> 00:32:38.340
And you know, it just took them a while to get there.

663
00:32:38.540 --> 00:32:39.400
Poor Dwight.

664
00:32:39.740 --> 00:32:41.600
He's having to listen to all this.

665
00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:44.040
And he tries to put Andy off.

666
00:32:44.160 --> 00:32:46.180
He says, I don't think we should talk about this.

667
00:32:46.320 --> 00:32:47.640
People might overhear us.

668
00:32:47.800 --> 00:32:52.260
But then Andy just takes that to mean that they should come up with code names.

669
00:32:53.280 --> 00:32:58.160
Dwight's like, okay, well, Angela can stay the same, but let's change Andy to Dwight.

670
00:32:58.620 --> 00:32:58.940
Hmm.

671
00:32:59.440 --> 00:32:59.780
Okay.

672
00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:00.320
Yeah.

673
00:33:00.480 --> 00:33:03.880
And Andy's like, that's not really, that's not how this works.

674
00:33:04.160 --> 00:33:11.680
Lady, this really reminded me of how you and I tried to come up with code names when we

675
00:33:11.680 --> 00:33:15.780
would have conversations and we were afraid other people could overhear us.

676
00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:19.060
And our code names were about this good.

677
00:33:19.220 --> 00:33:20.900
They were so obvious.

678
00:33:20.900 --> 00:33:23.360
It would be like a person's initials.

679
00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:24.600
So obvious.

680
00:33:24.980 --> 00:33:25.600
I know.

681
00:33:25.820 --> 00:33:27.860
We're not going to be spies, Jenna.

682
00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:32.920
We're not going to be spies, but we're going to be mom detectives solving small crimes.

683
00:33:32.960 --> 00:33:35.420
I would love to be a mom detective with you.

684
00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:37.020
When the pandemic is over.

685
00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:42.140
Mom detectives solving the small crimes no one cares about in your neighborhood.

686
00:33:43.520 --> 00:33:44.700
It's a hit.

687
00:33:45.140 --> 00:33:48.640
Who graffitied the construction cone at the intersection?

688
00:33:49.580 --> 00:33:50.060
Exactly.

689
00:33:50.640 --> 00:33:51.740
We'll find out.

690
00:33:52.100 --> 00:33:53.100
We'll tell you.

691
00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:54.020
We're on it.

692
00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:58.580
So coming up, we have a scene between Ryan and Michael that is so funny to me.

693
00:33:59.080 --> 00:34:02.440
When Michael calls him, you know, to gripe.

694
00:34:02.560 --> 00:34:04.020
First of all, he calls him Rye.

695
00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:07.860
Ryan is like, don't call me about small probleMs.

696
00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:09.180
What have I told you about that?

697
00:34:09.180 --> 00:34:12.640
Yeah, and he says, well, you don't like it when I call you about big problems either.

698
00:34:12.660 --> 00:34:13.980
So what do you want from me?

699
00:34:14.060 --> 00:34:14.640
What do you want?

700
00:34:14.880 --> 00:34:19.140
So Jason said on the DVD commentary that they shot this in real time.

701
00:34:19.540 --> 00:34:23.960
Now, Jenna, do you remember they had built a little office that was Ryan's office at

702
00:34:23.960 --> 00:34:24.740
corporate, right?

703
00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:25.220
Yes.

704
00:34:25.400 --> 00:34:29.860
So they had BJ, you know, on the other side of the soundstage in this little room with

705
00:34:29.860 --> 00:34:30.700
a camera with him.

706
00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:34.700
And then they had a camera with Steve in his office as Michael.

707
00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:36.840
And that's the room Jason sat in.

708
00:34:36.840 --> 00:34:38.340
And they shot it simultaneously.

709
00:34:38.800 --> 00:34:42.780
And I think it really works because the chemistry is happening in real time.

710
00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:47.920
I always loved when we did that because it meant that you could improvise a little and

711
00:34:47.920 --> 00:34:52.120
the other person could react to you and you would get it on both cameras.

712
00:34:52.420 --> 00:34:52.680
Yes.

713
00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:58.020
And BJ said something I thought was so delightful because it's one of my favorite lines in this

714
00:34:58.020 --> 00:34:58.720
whole episode.

715
00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:02.860
BJ said he wrote the line, I can't cook and I'm starting a restaurant.

716
00:35:02.860 --> 00:35:07.960
And the restaurant he says he's going to start is Mike's Cereal Shack.

717
00:35:08.100 --> 00:35:10.300
It's going to have every cereal you can get in the store.

718
00:35:10.540 --> 00:35:10.880
Yeah.

719
00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:16.080
I want you to know I did a little bit of a deep dive on Mike's Cereal Shack.

720
00:35:16.220 --> 00:35:17.140
I had to know.

721
00:35:17.280 --> 00:35:19.520
Has anyone opened a Cereal Shack?

722
00:35:20.320 --> 00:35:21.840
The answer is no.

723
00:35:21.840 --> 00:35:30.880
But a woman named Catherine has designed the most adorable vintage poster for Mike's Cereal

724
00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:31.380
Shack.

725
00:35:31.540 --> 00:35:35.540
She has a company on Etsy called Cat Bone Design.

726
00:35:35.540 --> 00:35:37.800
It's so, so cute.

727
00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:40.100
I'm going to put a picture of it in the pod.

728
00:35:40.240 --> 00:35:42.660
And then her profile really got me, Ange.

729
00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:48.600
Her profile says, I'm a stay-at-home mom with a passion for creating colorful and happy homes.

730
00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:51.760
Good design keeps me balanced and brings me joy.

731
00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:55.080
And being a designer means I can share that joy with others.

732
00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:56.820
Jenna shared it with me.

733
00:35:56.920 --> 00:35:57.560
It is adorable.

734
00:35:57.780 --> 00:36:01.100
We're going to put it in our stories, you guys, so you can look at the rest of her art.

735
00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:04.680
There's one more thing I want to say about this scene before we move on.

736
00:36:04.980 --> 00:36:05.880
Ryan's assistant.

737
00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:06.540
Yes.

738
00:36:06.700 --> 00:36:10.800
She pokes her head in the door at the beginning and she says, Eddie Murphy is on the line for

739
00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:11.020
you.

740
00:36:11.220 --> 00:36:11.500
Yes.

741
00:36:11.820 --> 00:36:14.760
Well, Ryan's assistant was played by Virginia Newcomb.

